{
  "spike": "S7",
  "date": "2026-10-04",
  "what": "P2.5 rate control and FEC over WebTransport under netem (spikes/s7-wan): the streamer in a container on gpu-node (RTX 4090) with the link shaped in its own network namespace, the bench page in the Claude desktop app's built-in browser (Chromium 152) on the M4 Pro (1 GbE). Test pattern at the bench page's size (about 1792×1008: the picture follows the page's element), 60 fps with 20 % of the picture noise (CHA_TESTPATTERN_NOISE=20), session ceiling 40 Mbit/s.",
  "notes": [
    "mbps: what the page received (video, parity and audio). fps: frames decoded per second. framesLost: frames dropped (unrecoverable, or waiting for the keyframe after one). framesRebuilt: frames FEC completed. maxGapMs: the longest wait between two frames in the phase.",
    "delivery: server send → frame complete in the page's worker, clock-synced by pings that assume symmetric paths; netem delays only the server → page direction, so the absolute values are off by about half the added delay. Differences within a run are exact.",
    "NVENC's low-latency CBR (one-frame VBV) makes about two thirds of its target on this picture: 24.5 Mbit/s at the 40 Mbit/s ceiling.",
    "Before P2.5 (same bench, fixed 40 Mbit/s, QUIC's congestion control): 3 % loss gave 10 fps and then 1 fps; a drop to 10 Mbit/s froze the picture for good.",
    "netem's jitter reordered packets in all runs but wan_hevc_in_order (each packet its own delay: at ~1 packet/ms with ±5 ms, nearly every packet out of order); netem.sh now keeps them in order unless S7_REORDER=1.",
    "WebRTC (Chrome's receiver, fixed 40 Mbit/s target before rate control) on wan with reordering: 29 fps, 15,788 NACKs for 284 packets lost, 48 freezes totalling 38 s in 75 s. With in-order jitter: 29 NACKs for 29 losses in 10 s, no freezes. str0m's GCC was tried and rejected: it paces media at 1.1x its estimate and its estimate stalled at 8-12 Mbit/s on unshaped 1 GbE."
  ],
